janet yellen is the first female chair  of the United States Federal Reserve the  most powerful central bank on the planet  she is a steadfast policy maker with a  thoughtful and careful approach she is  renowned for being calm and collected  under pressure and criticism this is a  brief history of Janet Yellen 1946  Yellen is born in Brooklyn New York her  father is a doctor her mother a  schoolteacher 1959 Yellen attends Fort  Hamilton High School in Bay Ridge  Brooklyn she collects rocks 1963 Yellen  graduates high school as valedictorian  in class scholar she attends Brown  University's then Women's College  Pembroke she plans to study philosophy  she later switches to economics 1967  Yellen graduates from Pembroke she moves  to Yale University for her PhD in  economics she studies under Nobel  laureate James Tobin who held the view  that government spending could alleviate  recession she's impressed by a sense of  social responsibility 1977 Yellen serves  as an economist with the Federal Reserve  Board of Governors he meets fellow  economist George Akerlof they marry the  next year and become lifelong  collaborators 1978 Yellen rejoins  academia coming a lecturer at the London  School of Economics and later University  of California Berkeley she receives two  excellence in teaching awards 1994 after  years of teaching Yellen takes leave  from UC Berkeley to serve on the Federal  Reserve Board of Governors under Alan  Greenspan she argues for more fed  transparency 1997 Yellen serves on the  White House Council of Economic Advisers  under President Clinton she helps the  President formulate his economic policy  2004 Yellen heads back to the Bay Area  this time to become president of the  Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco  2005 Yellen feels uneasy about the US  housing market saying quote the housing  sector represents a risk to the US  Outlook 2008 after she calls for the Fed  to act preemptively to protect the  economy Yellen  among the first Fed officials to call  the recession 2010 Yellen becomes vice  chair of the Federal Reserve job  creation must be a high priority of  monetary policy she is an outspoken  advocate for using the powers of the Fed  to reduce unemployment 2014 Yellen  succeeds Ben Bernanke as chair of the  Federal Reserve  she keeps interest rates near zero to  continue supporting the economy she  inspires the NYU class of 2014  congratulations and good luck 2015 the  majority of the year Yellen holds on to  the feds easiest monetary policy F she  plans to gradually unwind the feds  measures as the economy improves even  with surprises in global events Yellen  is sure to continue her evidence-based  approach to policy and keep calm and  carry on
 

